== the four tapes, as they arrive ==
  CU_A: Loan # | Balance | Int Rate | Orig Date
  CU_B: Note ID | Principal | Interest Rate | Origination
  CU_C: loan_number | Amount | Rate (%) | Date
  CU_D: Loan # | Int Rate

== consolidating ==
ok       = false   (false because a source was rejected)
accepted = CU_A, CU_B, CU_C
rejected = CU_D

what it decided, and why:
  CU_A.rate: inferred whole (a value exceeds 1, which no fraction rate can)
  CU_B.rate: AMBIGUOUS, assumed fraction (every value is at or below 1: fractions, or whole percents under 1% -- declare `scale` to be sure)
  CU_C.rate: inferred whole (the values carry a written % sign)
  CU_D: REJECTED, missing required column(s) balance

== one frame, one set of names, provenance on every row ==
columns: tape | loan_id | balance | rate
  CU_A  A-100  balance 150000  rate 0.0525
  CU_A  A-101  balance 82500.5  rate 0.04875
  CU_B  B-200  balance 1500  rate 0.0475
  CU_B  B-201  balance 23750.2  rate 0.0525
  CU_C  C-300  balance -1200  rate 0.06
  CU_C  C-301  balance 9000  rate 0.065

== every rate is now on ONE scale, whatever the tape wrote ==
  A-100 -> 5.25%
  A-101 -> 4.875%
  B-200 -> 4.75%
  B-201 -> 5.25%
  C-300 -> 6%
  C-301 -> 6.5%

== declaring beats inferring, and the report shows the difference ==
  CU_B.rate: scale declared as fraction

== the money forms, checked one by one ==
  1500        -> 1500
  "$1,500.00" -> 1500
  "(1,200.00)"-> -1200
  "1,200.00-" -> -1200
  "9,000"     -> 9000
  "n/a"       -> unknown

== name matching is normalised, so punctuation and case do not matter ==
  "Rate (%)" ~ "rate"        : true
  "Int. Rate" ~ "int rate"   : true
  "Loan #" ~ "loan"          : true
